WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.640
Good morning, John.

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:05.780
So, now that we've been regularly photographing the Earth from space for decades,

00:00:05.780 --> 00:00:12.080
we can watch in like time-lapse format, as our planet changes - usually, as we change our planet.

00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:14.900
Huge scale stuff. And Google has just released a tool that makes this easier.

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:22.440
Basically, you can scan around the entire world and watch the last 25 years of life on Earth.

00:00:22.440 --> 00:00:26.860
What the world was like when I was four versus what the world is like now.

00:00:26.860 --> 00:00:31.580
So I wanted to share some of the cool things that I found while doing that. First, here's my hometown of Missoula

00:00:31.580 --> 00:00:36.640
And if you look carefully, you can see some new neighborhoods being built and all the boxstores going into the edge of town.

00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:39.740
Bit of a small town but it has changed in the last 25 years.

00:00:39.740 --> 00:00:45.200
More interesting probably is the amazing sprawl of Orlando, where John, you and I grew up.

00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:49.200
But really, the most fascinating bits are where humans have had their deepest influences.

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:55.040
America's insatiable appetite for cheap coal power, our wonderful lifestyles has lead to a practice

00:00:55.040 --> 00:00:58.040
called mountain top removal mining, in much of Appalachia.

00:00:58.040 --> 00:01:03.440
I've seen these pictures, I've seen close-ups and I've seen it from satellites but as you scroll around and watch

00:01:03.500 --> 00:01:14.100
the last 20 years' progress, it is astounding and terrifying and moving to see the amount of destruction.

00:01:14.200 --> 00:01:17.740
And of course, I know that I benefit personally from this destruction but it is destruction.

00:01:17.740 --> 00:01:23.860
Similarly, we all know that lots of the Amazon rainforests has been cut down but you really can't understand

00:01:23.900 --> 00:01:32.480
the depth and the scale of it until you watch it happen and are able to move all around Brazil and Bolivia and see how

00:01:32.480 --> 00:01:34.480
much of those forests are gone now.

00:01:34.480 --> 00:01:38.860
And then there's the story of water, which of course, more people consume more of.

00:01:38.900 --> 00:01:46.700
Las Vegas and Dubai spreading across deserts, Saudi Arabia, with massive irrigation projects making the deserts bloom.

00:01:46.700 --> 00:01:53.700
Inland seas drying up, either because of drought or because of irrigation but in addition to being terrifying occasionally, it can also be a story of recovery.

00:01:53.800 --> 00:02:01.420
Watching the forests take back the land that had been destroyed when Mt. Saint Helens erupted was particularly inspiring, though the nearby clear cutting was not.

00:02:01.420 --> 00:02:06.740
We humans have a profound and largely negative effect on the rest of the lifeforms of the planet.

00:02:06.900 --> 00:02:13.900
Science has, for a long time offered us these truths up on a platter in the form of data and numbers and statistics.

00:02:13.900 --> 00:02:18.780
But we are people, we are not computers and we are not particularly good at understanding

00:02:18.780 --> 00:02:21.160
what all of those data and statistics really mean.

00:02:21.160 --> 00:02:26.260
And it might be a better world if all policy was based on science, but it's not. It's based on the individual

00:02:26.260 --> 00:02:31.060
decisions and the individual feelings of individual people, like ME and like YOU.

00:02:31.060 --> 00:02:36.800
For me, watching all of this change with a very limited span of my own life is intense and it's moving

00:02:36.800 --> 00:02:41.980
and it's terrifying. We have learned a lot but we haven't really acted on that learning.

00:02:41.980 --> 00:02:48.700
And maybe that's because we don't really understand it. We know the numbers but we can't see it, or we couldn't see it.

00:02:48.700 --> 00:02:55.400
Maybe taking a look around the Google Earth engine, which I should say, is based on the NASA LandSat program, which is FANTASTIC, good job, NASA.

00:02:55.400 --> 00:03:01.660
It might give us all a better understanding of the realities that we face and if we really understand those things better,

00:03:01.660 --> 00:03:03.940
then the decisions we make will be better.

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:05.860
At least one can hope.

00:03:05.860 --> 00:03:10.700
So YAY for NASA, YAY for Google, YAY for Science, YAY for understanding and hopefully,

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:13.660
also in the near future, YAY, for action.

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:15.760
John, I'll see you on Tuesday.

